window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.3","title":"The Central Limit Theorem","goal":"Show that sample means from a very skewed population become more symmetric and bell-shaped as the sample size grows from 1 to 5 to 30.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"A skewed population","text":"Waiting times in a clinic: exponential, with a mean of 10 minutes and an SD of 10. Lots of short waits and a long tail of rare long ones, so it is clearly skewed to the right.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(99)\n\ndef sample_means(n, reps=5000):\n    return rng.exponential(10, size=(reps, n)).mean(axis=1)\n\nsingle = rng.exponential(10, size=5000)\nprint(f'skewness of single waits = {stats.skew(single):.2f}')","out":"skewness of single waits = 1.89","figs":[]},{"title":"Skewness of the sample means","text":"Skewness is 0 for a perfectly symmetric shape. For each n we take 5,000 sample means and measure their skewness. It falls towards 0 roughly like 2 divided by the square root of n.","code":"print('  n   skewness of means   2/sqrt(n)')\nfor n in (1, 5, 30):\n    m = sample_means(n)\n    print(f'{n:3d}   {stats.skew(m):15.2f}   {2 / np.sqrt(n):9.2f}')","out":"  n   skewness of means   2/sqrt(n)\n  1              1.93        2.00\n  5              0.85        0.89\n 30              0.33        0.37","figs":[]},{"title":"Three histograms","text":"The same story as a picture. With n = 1 we see the skewed population itself. With n = 5 the shape is already more balanced, and with n = 30 it looks close to a bell.","code":"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(6, 3.4), sharex=True)\nfor ax, n in zip(axes, (1, 5, 30)):\n    ax.hist(sample_means(n), bins=30, color='steelblue', edgecolor='white')\n    ax.set_title(f'n = {n}')\n    ax.set_yticks([])\naxes[1].set_xlabel('sample mean (minutes)')\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":["4.3-3.png"]},{"title":"Use the bell curve","text":"The lesson's clinic question: for n = 40 patients, how often does the mean wait reach 12 minutes or more? The CLT lets us use a normal curve with mean 10 and SE = 10 divided by the square root of 40.","code":"se = 10 / np.sqrt(40)\np_clt = stats.norm.sf(12, loc=10, scale=se)\nsimulated = (sample_means(40, reps=20000) >= 12).mean()\n\nprint(f'SE = {se:.3f}')\nprint(f'CLT answer       P(mean >= 12) = {p_clt:.3f}')\nprint(f'simulation (20k) P(mean >= 12) = {simulated:.3f}')","out":"SE = 1.581\nCLT answer       P(mean >= 12) = 0.103\nsimulation (20k) P(mean >= 12) = 0.110","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"skew <- function(x) mean((x - mean(x))^3) / mean((x - mean(x))^2)^1.5\nset.seed(99)\nfor (n in c(1, 5, 30)) print(skew(replicate(5000, mean(rexp(n, rate = 1/10)))))\npnorm(12, mean = 10, sd = 10 / sqrt(40), lower.tail = FALSE)","expect":"skewness about 1.9, 0.9, 0.35 for n = 1, 5, 30 (Python: 1.93, 0.85, 0.33); pnorm answer 0.1029","note":"Base R has no skewness function, so we write the same formula SciPy uses. Simulated skewness values are close to Python's, not identical; the pnorm answer is exact."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives > Options > Skewness","syntax":"SET SEED=99.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP s = 1 TO 5000.\nCOMPUTE x1 = RV.EXP(1/10).\nCOMPUTE total = 0.\nLOOP i = 1 TO 30.\nCOMPUTE total = total + RV.EXP(1/10).\nEND LOOP.\nCOMPUTE mean30 = total/30.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=x1 mean30 /STATISTICS=MEAN STDDEV SKEWNESS.","note":"x1 is a single wait (n = 1) and mean30 is a mean of 30 waits, so you can compare their skewness. SPSS uses a slightly adjusted skewness formula and its own random numbers, so values are close to Python's, not identical."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset seed 99\nset obs 5000\ngenerate x1 = rexponential(10)\ngenerate mean30 = 0\nforvalues i = 1/30 {\n    quietly replace mean30 = mean30 + rexponential(10) / 30\n}\ntabstat x1 mean30, statistics(mean sd skewness)\ndisplay 1 - normal((12 - 10) / (10 / sqrt(40)))","note":"Stata's skewness uses the same formula as SciPy. Simulated values are close to Python's but not identical; the last line gives the exact normal answer of 0.103."},"try":["Add n = 100 to the loop and predict whether the skewness will be above or below 0.2.","Make the population even more skewed with rng.lognormal(0, 1.2, ...) in place of the exponential. How big must n be before the means look symmetric?","Change the clinic cut-off from 12 to 11 minutes and predict the new probability."]});
